Chapter: ScopeViolation

  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 A capability effect may carry a scope literal naming the resources the
 grant covers. This opening promises it reads only under "/config/" and
 then reaches for /etc/passwd. The scope literal has always parsed and
 ridden the effect label; until CDX4002 was raised, nothing read it, so
 this program compiled and the promise meant nothing.

 A scope may narrow, never widen.

 The scope check keys on the name of the operation, not on whether the
 compiler happens to emit it as a builtin. Moving file-exists out of the
 emitter and into Foreword chapter Fat16 -- where a question about a
 filesystem belongs -- therefore leaves this refusal exactly where it
 was, and proving that is now part of what this test is for.

Section: Body
  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read "/config/", Device.Block] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (if file-exists "/etc/passwd" then "read it" else "absent")
  end
